'use strict';
/* Picture Classes for the data science course, part 17: pandas (dates, text and time series; loc, iloc and SQL side by side) and matplotlib (3D, seaborn and storytelling, heatmaps and animation). The plots are checked by reading back what was drawn. Outputs marked `@@` come from real runs (tools/verify_picture.js --fill). */

PIC.lesson({
  id: 'pd-dates', mod: 'pandas', les: [6], group: 'Data science and AI', title: 'Dates, text and time series', icon: '📅',
  setup: `import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nidx = pd.date_range("2024-01-01", periods=120, freq="D")\nsales = pd.Series(100 + 15 * (idx.dayofweek >= 5) + np.arange(120) * 0.3 + rng.normal(0, 5, 120), index=idx).round(1)`,
  sub: 'Turn messy date strings into real dates, group by week and month, smooth a noisy series with a rolling average, clean text with .str, compare with the same day last week, and find and fill missing days.',
  story: `Real tables are full of dates and words. A date stored as text cannot be sorted, subtracted or grouped by month; a column of product names with stray spaces and mixed capitals cannot be counted. pandas has a family of tools (<code>.dt</code> for dates, <code>.str</code> for text, <code>resample</code> and <code>rolling</code> for time) that turn both into something you can analyse. The data here are 120 days of simulated daily sales with a weekly rhythm (higher at weekends) and an upward trend.`,
  steps: [
    { code: `raw = pd.Series(["2024-03-05", "05/03/2024", "March 5, 2024", "2024/03/05 14:30"])\nparsed = pd.to_datetime(raw, format="mixed", dayfirst=True)\nprint(parsed.dt.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M").tolist())\nd = parsed[0]\nprint(d.year, d.month_name(), d.day_name(), d.dayofweek, (pd.Timestamp("2024-12-25") - d).days, "days until 25 December")`, out: `['2024-03-05 00:00', '2024-03-05 00:00', '2024-03-05 00:00', '2024-03-05 14:30']\n2024 March Tuesday 1 295 days until 25 December`, say: `<b>Parsing dates.</b> Four strings that all mean the same day, written four ways ("2024-03-05", "05/03/2024", "March 5, 2024" and "2024/03/05 14:30"), become real timestamps with <code>pd.to_datetime</code>. <code>format="mixed"</code> reads each on its own terms and <code>dayfirst=True</code> says that <b>05/03 means 5 March</b>, not 3 May: always state the order for ambiguous dates, because a wrong guess looks perfectly valid. Once they are real dates, the <code>.dt</code> tools work: the year (2024), the month name (March), the day name (Tuesday), the day number of the week (1, counting Monday as 0), and the difference between two dates, <b>295 days</b> from 5 March to 25 December. Convert dates <b>first</b>: everything below depends on it.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['written as', 'parsed as'], rows: [['2024-03-05', '2024-03-05 00:00'], ['05/03/2024 (day first)', '2024-03-05 00:00'], ['March 5, 2024', '2024-03-05 00:00'], ['2024/03/05 14:30', '2024-03-05 14:30']], hlRows: [1], cap: `one date, four spellings` } },
    { code: `print(sales.head(3).tolist(), "<- the first three days")\nprint(sales.index.dayofweek[:7].tolist(), "= Monday to Sunday")\nweekly = sales.resample("W").sum()\nmonthly = sales.resample("MS").mean().round(1)\nprint(len(weekly), "weeks;", weekly.head(2).round(1).tolist())\nprint({d.strftime("%b"): v for d, v in monthly.items()})`, out: `[100.6, 99.6, 103.8] <- the first three days\n[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] = Monday to Sunday\n18 weeks; [745.5, 730.3]\n{'Jan': 107.6, 'Feb': 119.2, 'Mar': 128.1, 'Apr': 135.7}`, say: `<b>Resampling: change the time scale.</b> The first three days of sales are 100.6, 99.6 and 103.8 (Monday to Wednesday; <code>dayofweek</code> numbers the days 0 to 6). <code>resample("W")</code> groups the days into <b>weeks</b> (18 of them, ending on Sundays) and adds them up: <b>745.5</b> and 730.3 for the first two weeks. <code>resample("MS")</code> groups by <b>month</b> (the "MS" means month start) and takes the average: <b>Jan 107.6, Feb 119.2, Mar 128.1, Apr 135.7</b>, showing the upward trend that the daily numbers hide in their noise. Resampling is <code>groupby</code> for time: choose the new frequency, then choose how to combine (sum for amounts that add up, mean for rates and prices).`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['period', 'result'], rows: [['first week (sum)', 745.5], ['second week (sum)', 730.3], ['January (mean per day)', 107.6], ['February', 119.2], ['March', 128.1], ['April (a partial month: 29 days)', 135.7]], cap: `sum amounts, average rates` } },
    { code: `roll = sales.rolling(7).mean()\nprint(int(roll.isna().sum()), "empty at the start;", roll.iloc[6:9].round(1).tolist())\nprint("a single day varies:", round(float(sales.std()), 1), "| the 7-day average varies:", round(float(roll.std()), 1))\ncentred = sales.rolling(7, center=True).mean()\nprint(round(float(sales["2024-01-04":"2024-01-10"].mean()), 2), round(float(centred["2024-01-07"]), 2))`, out: `6 empty at the start; [106.5, 107.4, 107.3]\na single day varies: 13.5 | the 7-day average varies: 10.2\n106.23 106.23`, say: `<b>A rolling average smooths the noise.</b> <code>rolling(7).mean()</code> is the average of each day and the six before it: the first <b>6 days are empty</b> (not enough history) and then 106.5, 107.4, 107.3. A single day varies by <b>13.5</b> (standard deviation), the 7-day average by only <b>10.2</b>: the weekly bump and the random noise have been averaged away and the trend is easier to see. Seven days is not an accident: a window of exactly one week averages every weekday once, so the weekend effect cancels. With <code>center=True</code> the window is placed around each day instead of behind it; the last line checks that the centred value for 7 January (106.23) equals the plain average of 4 to 10 January (106.23). A trailing window is what you can compute <b>today</b>; a centred one is nicer for a chart of the past.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['days with no rolling value at the start', 6], ['one day: standard deviation', 13.5], ['7-day average: standard deviation', 10.2], ['centred average for 7 Jan = mean of 4 to 10 Jan', 106.23]], cap: `a window of one week cancels the weekly rhythm` } },
    { code: `df = pd.DataFrame({"product": ["  Blue Pen ", "RED pen", "blue  notebook", "Green Notebook", "red PEN"], "qty": [3, 1, 2, 4, 5]})\nclean = df["product"].str.strip().str.lower().str.split().str.join(" ")\ndf["colour"] = clean.str.split().str[0]\ndf["kind"] = clean.str.split().str[-1]\nprint(clean.tolist())\nprint(df.groupby("kind").qty.sum().to_dict(), clean.str.contains("pen").tolist())\nprint(clean.str.extract("(blue|red|green)")[0].tolist())`, out: `['blue pen', 'red pen', 'blue notebook', 'green notebook', 'red pen']\n{'notebook': 6, 'pen': 9} [True, True, False, False, True]\n['blue', 'red', 'blue', 'green', 'red']`, say: `<b>Text is a column too.</b> Five product names arrive as "  Blue Pen ", "RED pen", "blue  notebook", "Green Notebook" and "red PEN": five spellings of three things. A chain of <code>.str</code> methods cleans them: <code>.strip()</code> removes the outer spaces, <code>.lower()</code> unifies the case, and <code>.split().str.join(" ")</code> collapses the double space: <b>blue pen, red pen, blue notebook, green notebook, red pen</b>. Now <code>.str.split().str[-1]</code> takes the last word as the <b>kind</b> and <code>.str[0]</code> the first as the colour; grouping by kind gives <b>notebook 6, pen 9</b> units. <code>.str.contains("pen")</code> gives True, True, False, False, True, and <code>.str.extract("(blue|red|green)")</code> pulls the colour out with a pattern (a regular expression). Clean first, count second: counting uncleaned text splits one product into many.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['raw', 'cleaned', 'kind'], rows: [['  Blue Pen ', 'blue pen', 'pen'], ['RED pen', 'red pen', 'pen'], ['blue  notebook', 'blue notebook', 'notebook'], ['Green Notebook', 'green notebook', 'notebook'], ['red PEN', 'red pen', 'pen']], cap: `clean, then group` } },
    { code: `last_week = sales.shift(7)\nchange = (sales / last_week - 1).dropna()\nprint(len(sales), len(change), "days can be compared with the same day a week earlier")\nprint(round(float(change.mean()), 3), round(float(change.min()), 3), round(float(change.max()), 3))\nprint(sales.diff().dropna().abs().max().round(1), "is the biggest one-day jump")`, out: `120 113 days can be compared with the same day a week earlier\n0.02 -0.124 0.177\n27.5 is the biggest one-day jump`, say: `<b>Compare with the same day last week.</b> <code>shift(7)</code> moves the whole series seven days later, so each day lines up with the same weekday a week earlier. Dividing and subtracting 1 gives the <b>week-over-week change</b>: of 120 days, <b>113</b> can be compared (the first seven have no earlier week). On average a day is <b>2%</b> above its counterpart a week earlier (the upward trend), ranging from <b>−12.4% to +17.7%</b>. Comparing like with like (Tuesday with Tuesday) avoids the trap of saying "Monday is lower than Saturday" when it is only the weekly rhythm. <code>diff()</code> gives the plain day-to-day change instead: the biggest single jump is <b>27.5</b>, from a weekday to a weekend. Use week-over-week for anything with a weekly pattern, and year-over-year for anything with a yearly one.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['days that can be compared with a week earlier', '113 of 120'], ['average change, week over week', '+2.0%'], ['smallest and largest change', '-12.4% to +17.7%'], ['biggest day-to-day jump', 27.5]], cap: `compare like with like` } },
    { code: `gappy = sales.drop(sales.index[[12, 13, 40]])\nprint(len(gappy), "rows;", len(gappy.index.to_series().diff().dropna().loc[lambda s: s > pd.Timedelta("1D")]), "gaps in the dates")\nfull = gappy.asfreq("D")\nprint(int(full.isna().sum()), "missing days after reindexing to every day")\nfilled = full.interpolate()\nprint([round(float(filled[d]), 1) for d in ("2024-01-13", "2024-01-14")], "filled in, against what really happened:", [float(sales[d]) for d in ("2024-01-13", "2024-01-14")])`, out: `117 rows; 2 gaps in the dates\n3 missing days after reindexing to every day\n[101.7, 99.8] filled in, against what really happened: [107.0, 117.8]`, say: `<b>Missing days.</b> Three days were dropped from the data (two gaps): 117 rows remain and <code>diff()</code> on the dates finds <b>2 gaps</b>. <code>asfreq("D")</code> puts the series back on a complete daily calendar, with <b>3 empty days</b> where there was no row, so nothing is silently skipped in a rolling average or a chart. <code>interpolate()</code> fills them with a straight line between the neighbours: 101.7 and 99.8 for 13 and 14 January, against what really happened, <b>107.0 and 117.8</b>. The fill is far too low: those two days were a Saturday and a Sunday, which are normally higher, and a straight line knows nothing about weekends. Filling is a <b>modelling decision</b>: for a trend-like series a line is fine, for a weekly pattern use the same weekday of the previous week, and always say what you did and how many values were filled.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['rows after dropping 3 days', 117], ['gaps found in the dates', 2], ['missing days after asfreq("D")', 3], ['filled in by a straight line (Sat, Sun)', '101.7 and 99.8'], ['what really happened', '107.0 and 117.8']], hlRows: [3, 4], cap: `a straight line does not know about weekends` } },
  ],
  quiz: { q: `A series has a strong weekly pattern. How should you compute its growth?`, opts: [`Compare each day with the previous day`, `Compare each day with the same weekday one week earlier (shift(7)), or use a 7-day window`, `Ignore dates`, `Use only Mondays`], a: 1, why: `Day-to-day changes mix the trend with the weekly rhythm. Same-weekday comparisons and 7-day windows remove the rhythm and show the trend.` },
  remember: [`Convert text to dates first with pd.to_datetime (state dayfirst for ambiguous dates); then use .dt.`, `resample("W") or ("MS") changes the time scale; sum amounts, average rates.`, `rolling(7).mean() smooths noise; a window of one week cancels the weekly rhythm.`, `Clean text with .str.strip().lower() before counting; .str.contains and .str.extract find patterns.`, `shift(7) compares with last week; asfreq("D") exposes missing days; filling them is a modelling choice.`],
  lab: { href: '#/notebook', label: 'Try it in the Notebook' },
});

PIC.lesson({
  id: 'pd-loc', mod: 'pandas', les: [7], group: 'Data science and AI', title: 'loc, iloc and SQL side by side', icon: '🪞',
  setup: `import sqlite3\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\norders = pd.DataFrame({"order_id": [101, 102, 103, 104, 105, 106, 107, 108], "customer": ["asha", "ben", "asha", "chitra", "ben", "asha", "dev", "chitra"],\n                       "product": ["pen", "book", "lamp", "book", "pen", "desk", "pen", "lamp"], "qty": [3, 1, 2, 5, 4, 1, 2, 6], "price": [1.5, 12.0, 20.0, 12.0, 1.5, 150.0, 1.5, 20.0]},\n                      index=list("abcdefgh"))\ncon = sqlite3.connect(":memory:")\norders.to_sql("orders", con, index=False)\ncustomers = pd.DataFrame({"customer": ["asha", "ben", "chitra", "eve"], "city": ["Pune", "Delhi", "Pune", "Goa"]})\ncustomers.to_sql("customers", con, index=False)\ndef sql(query):\n    return pd.read_sql(query, con)`,
  sub: 'Pick rows and columns by label or by position, and see that the same question asked with pandas and with SQL gives the same answer: filter, sort and limit, group, and join. Also avoid the chained-assignment trap.',
  story: `pandas and SQL answer the same questions in two languages. If you know one, you can read the other. This lesson puts them <b>side by side</b>: every pandas answer is checked against a real SQL query run on the same data in an in-memory SQLite database, and they agree. First, the two ways of picking: <code>loc</code> by <i>label</i> and <code>iloc</code> by <i>position</i>, which differ in a way that catches nearly everybody once. The data are eight small orders; the row labels are the letters a to h, so that label and position are different things.`,
  steps: [
    { code: `print(orders.loc["c", "product"], orders.iloc[2]["product"])\nprint(len(orders.loc["b":"d"]), "rows with loc (both ends included)", "|", len(orders.iloc[1:3]), "rows with iloc (the end is left out)")\nprint(orders.loc["b":"d", ["product", "qty"]].to_dict("list"))\nprint(orders.iloc[[0, -1], 0].tolist())`, out: `lamp lamp\n3 rows with loc (both ends included) | 2 rows with iloc (the end is left out)\n{'product': ['book', 'lamp', 'book'], 'qty': [1, 2, 5]}\n[101, 108]`, say: `<b><code>loc</code> uses labels, <code>iloc</code> uses positions.</b> The row labelled "c" is also the third row (position 2): <code>orders.loc["c", "product"]</code> and <code>orders.iloc[2]["product"]</code> both give <b>lamp</b>. The difference that bites: a <b>label slice includes both ends</b>, <code>loc["b":"d"]</code> gives <b>3 rows</b> (b, c and d); a <b>position slice leaves the end out</b>, <code>iloc[1:3]</code> gives <b>2 rows</b> (positions 1 and 2), like a normal Python slice. <code>loc</code> also takes column names: <code>loc["b":"d", ["product", "qty"]]</code> gives books 1, lamp 2, book 5. And <code>iloc[[0, -1], 0]</code> takes the first and the last row of the first column (101 and 108): negative positions count from the end. When the index is the default 0, 1, 2 ... the two look alike, which hides the difference until the index changes (after sorting or filtering).`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['you write', 'rows or values', 'rule'], rows: [['orders.loc["b":"d"]', '3 rows', 'labels: both ends included'], ['orders.iloc[1:3]', '2 rows', 'positions: the end is left out'], ['orders.loc["c", "product"]', 'lamp', 'by label'], ['orders.iloc[2]["product"]', 'lamp', 'by position']], hlRows: [0, 1], cap: `label slices include the end` } },
    { code: `mask = (orders.qty >= 2) & (orders.price > 10)\nvia_pandas = orders[mask].order_id.tolist()\nvia_sql = sql("select order_id from orders where qty >= 2 and price > 10")["order_id"].tolist()\nprint(via_pandas, via_sql, via_pandas == via_sql)`, out: `[103, 104, 108] [103, 104, 108] True`, say: `<b>Filtering = WHERE.</b> The pandas filter <code>orders[(orders.qty >= 2) & (orders.price > 10)]</code> and the SQL <code>select order_id from orders where qty >= 2 and price > 10</code> pick the <b>same three orders: 103, 104 and 108</b> (the check prints <code>True</code>). The two translate word for word: a condition on columns, <code>&amp;</code> in pandas (with brackets around each side) where SQL says <code>and</code>, <code>|</code> for <code>or</code>, <code>~</code> for <code>not</code>. If a pandas filter returns something surprising, write it as a SQL <code>where</code> in words; if a SQL query is unclear, write it as the pandas mask. Moving between the two languages is a useful way to check yourself.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['pandas', 'SQL'], rows: [['orders[mask]', 'select ... from orders where ...'], ['(qty >= 2) & (price > 10)', 'qty >= 2 and price > 10'], ['either: orders 103, 104, 108', 'the same three orders']], hlRows: [2], cap: `two spellings of one question` } },
    { code: `top_pandas = orders.loc[:, ["product", "qty"]].sort_values("qty", ascending=False).head(3)\ntop_sql = sql("select product, qty from orders order by qty desc limit 3")\nprint(top_pandas.values.tolist(), top_sql.values.tolist())\nprint(top_pandas.values.tolist() == top_sql.values.tolist())`, out: `[['lamp', 6], ['book', 5], ['pen', 4]] [['lamp', 6], ['book', 5], ['pen', 4]]\nTrue`, say: `<b>Columns, ordering and limits.</b> The pandas chain <code>orders.loc[:, ["product", "qty"]].sort_values("qty", ascending=False).head(3)</code> and the SQL <code>select product, qty from orders order by qty desc limit 3</code> give <b>lamp 6, book 5, pen 4</b> in both. <code>loc[:, columns]</code> is <code>select</code> (the colon means "all rows"), <code>sort_values</code> is <code>order by</code>, <code>head(3)</code> is <code>limit 3</code>. One caution: when two rows tie on the sort column, the order between them is <b>not defined</b> in either system, so add a second sort key if the answer must be reproducible (the data here have no ties on quantity at the top).`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['pandas', 'SQL', 'meaning'], rows: [['loc[:, ["product", "qty"]]', 'select product, qty', 'choose the columns'], ['sort_values("qty", ascending=False)', 'order by qty desc', 'sort'], ['head(3)', 'limit 3', 'the first three']], cap: `the same answer: lamp 6, book 5, pen 4` } },
    { code: `by_pandas = orders.assign(total=orders.qty * orders.price).groupby("customer").total.sum().round(2).to_dict()\nby_sql = sql("select customer, sum(qty * price) as total from orders group by customer").set_index("customer")["total"].round(2).to_dict()\nprint(by_pandas)\nprint(by_pandas == by_sql)`, out: `{'asha': 194.5, 'ben': 18.0, 'chitra': 180.0, 'dev': 3.0}\nTrue`, say: `<b>Grouping = GROUP BY.</b> Total spend per customer: <code>orders.assign(total=orders.qty * orders.price).groupby("customer").total.sum()</code> and <code>select customer, sum(qty * price) from orders group by customer</code> both give <b>asha 194.5, ben 18.0, chitra 180.0, dev 3.0</b>, and the comparison prints <code>True</code>. Read the pandas chain as the SQL clauses: <code>assign</code> computes the new column (the expression inside <code>sum(...)</code>), <code>groupby</code> is <code>group by</code>, <code>.sum()</code> is the aggregate. For several aggregates at once use <code>.agg()</code> in pandas or several columns in <code>select</code>. A good habit when a groupby result looks strange: write down the SQL and see which clause you got wrong.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['customer', 'total spent'], rows: [['asha', 194.5], ['ben', 18.0], ['chitra', 180.0], ['dev', 3.0]], hlRows: [0], cap: `groupby and group by agree` } },
    { code: `inner = orders.merge(customers, on="customer", how="inner")\nleft = orders.merge(customers, on="customer", how="left")\nright = customers.merge(orders, on="customer", how="left")\nprint(len(inner), len(left), len(right), "rows; customers with no orders:", right[right.order_id.isna()].customer.tolist())\nprint(len(sql("select * from orders o join customers c on o.customer = c.customer")), len(sql("select * from customers c left join orders o on o.customer = c.customer")))\nprint(left[left.city.isna()].customer.tolist(), "have no city in the customers table")`, out: `7 8 8 rows; customers with no orders: ['eve']\n7 8\n['dev'] have no city in the customers table`, say: `<b>Joining.</b> The orders table meets the customers table (four customers, one of them, "eve", without any order; and "dev" has orders but is not in the customers table). <code>merge(how="inner")</code> keeps only the matches: <b>7</b> rows (dev's order disappears). <code>how="left"</code> keeps every order: <b>8</b> rows, with an empty city for dev. Starting from the customers table, a left merge gives <b>8</b> rows and shows <b>eve</b> with no order. SQL agrees: the <code>join</code> returns 7 rows and the <code>left join</code> from customers returns 8. Before any join, ask two questions: what should happen to rows with no match (inner drops them, left keeps them with empty values), and is the key unique on both sides (a repeated key multiplies rows)? Count rows before and after every merge: a surprise there is the most common join bug.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['join', 'rows', 'who is lost or empty'], rows: [['inner (orders and customers)', 7, 'dev\'s order drops out'], ['left (orders first)', 8, 'dev has no city'], ['left (customers first)', 8, 'eve has no order'], ['SQL join / left join', '7 / 8', 'the same counts']], cap: `count rows before and after` } },
    { code: `copy = orders.copy()\ncopy[copy.qty > 3]["price"] = 0                       # a chained assignment: it changes a temporary copy\nprint((copy.price == 0).sum(), "prices changed by the chained assignment")\ncopy.loc[copy.qty > 3, "price"] = 0                   # one loc call, rows and column together\nprint((copy.price == 0).sum(), "prices changed by .loc")\nprint(orders.price.tolist() == [1.5, 12.0, 20.0, 12.0, 1.5, 150.0, 1.5, 20.0])`, out: `0 prices changed by the chained assignment\n3 prices changed by .loc\nTrue`, say: `<b>A classic trap: chained assignment.</b> <code>copy[copy.qty > 3]["price"] = 0</code> looks like "set the price of the big orders to zero", but it does two steps: first <code>copy[...]</code> builds a <i>temporary</i> table of the matching rows, then sets a column <b>of that temporary table</b>, which is thrown away. The check shows <b>0 prices changed</b>. The correct way is one <code>.loc</code> call that names the rows and the column together: <code>copy.loc[copy.qty > 3, "price"] = 0</code> changes <b>3 prices</b> (the orders with 5, 4 and 6 units). pandas often prints a warning about this, but not always, and the silent failure is the worse case. Rule: <b>to assign, use <code>.loc[rows, column] = value</code></b>; never index twice in a row on the left of an equals sign. (The original table is untouched because we worked on a copy.)`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['code', 'prices changed'], rows: [['copy[copy.qty > 3]["price"] = 0  (two steps)', 0], ['copy.loc[copy.qty > 3, "price"] = 0  (one step)', 3]], hlRows: [1], cap: `assign with one .loc` } },
  ],
  quiz: { q: `orders.loc["b":"d"] and orders.iloc[1:3] both start at the second row. How many rows does each return?`, opts: [`2 and 2`, `3 and 3`, `3 and 2: a label slice includes the last label, a position slice leaves out the end position`, `2 and 3`], a: 2, why: `loc slices by label and includes both ends; iloc slices by position like ordinary Python and excludes the end.` },
  remember: [`loc selects by label (both ends included); iloc by position (the end is left out).`, `A boolean mask is a WHERE clause; sort_values is ORDER BY; head is LIMIT; groupby is GROUP BY; merge is JOIN.`, `Check pandas against SQL (or the reverse) when you are unsure: they must agree.`, `Count rows before and after every merge; decide what happens to unmatched rows.`, `Assign with a single .loc[rows, column] = value, never with two indexings in a row.`],
  lab: { href: '#/notebook', label: 'Try it in the Notebook' },
});

PIC.lesson({
  id: 'mpl-3d', mod: 'matplotlib', les: [3], group: 'Data science and AI', title: 'Seeing in 3D', icon: '🧊',
  setup: `import io\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use("Agg")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom mpl_toolkits.mplot3d import proj3d`,
  sub: 'Build a surface from a grid, draw it with matplotlib, see how the same point lands in different places as you turn the view, find the angle that separates two clusters, learn what a 3D view hides and save the picture. Runs on your own computer.',
  story: `A 3D plot puts a third number on a picture. It is striking and, used carelessly, <b>misleading</b>: a flat screen can only show a 3D object from <i>one</i> viewing angle at a time, and the angle changes what you see. This lesson draws a real surface with matplotlib (running without a window, so everything can be measured), shows by arithmetic how the view changes the picture, and ends with when 3D helps and when a flat chart is better. It needs matplotlib on your own computer.`,
  steps: [
    { code: `x = np.linspace(-6, 6, 61)\ny = np.linspace(-6, 6, 61)\nX, Y = np.meshgrid(x, y)\nZ = np.sin(np.sqrt(X ** 2 + Y ** 2))\nprint(X.shape, Z.shape, round(float(Z.min()), 3), round(float(Z.max()), 3), round(float(Z[30, 30]), 3))\nprint(X[0, :3].round(1).tolist(), Y[:3, 0].round(1).tolist())`, out: `(61, 61) (61, 61) -1.0 1.0 0.0\n[-6.0, -5.8, -5.6] [-6.0, -5.8, -5.6]`, say: `<b>A surface is a grid of heights.</b> <code>np.linspace(-6, 6, 61)</code> makes 61 evenly spaced values for x and for y; <code>np.meshgrid</code> turns them into two <b>61 × 61 grids</b> in which every cell knows its own x and y. Then one line computes a height for every cell at once: <code>Z = sin(sqrt(X² + Y²))</code>, ripples spreading out from the centre like rings on water. The heights run from <b>−1.0 to 1.0</b>, the centre cell <code>Z[30, 30]</code> (where x and y are both 0) is <b>0.0</b>, and the printed first values of the grids show x counting up along a row (−6.0, −5.8, −5.6) and y counting up down a column. Every 3D surface starts like this: two coordinate grids and a height grid of the same shape.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['x and y values', '61 each, from -6 to 6'], ['grid shape', '61 x 61'], ['heights Z = sin(sqrt(x² + y²))', 'from -1.0 to 1.0'], ['the centre cell', 0.0]], hlRows: [1], cap: `two coordinate grids and a height grid` } },
    { code: `fig = plt.figure(figsize=(5, 4))\nax = fig.add_subplot(projection="3d")\nsurface = ax.plot_surface(X, Y, Z, cmap="viridis")\nax.set_xlabel("x"); ax.set_ylabel("y"); ax.set_zlabel("height")\nprint(type(ax).__name__, "default view: elevation", ax.elev, "azimuth", ax.azim)\nprint(type(surface).__name__, "with", len(surface.get_facecolors()), "coloured faces (60 x 60 grid squares)")`, out: `Axes3D default view: elevation 30 azimuth -60\nPoly3DCollection with 900 coloured faces (60 x 60 grid squares)`, say: `<b>Draw it.</b> <code>fig.add_subplot(projection="3d")</code> gives a 3D axes; <code>plot_surface(X, Y, Z, cmap="viridis")</code> draws the surface, colouring by height. The default viewing position is an <b>elevation of 30 degrees</b> (how far above the surface you look from) and an <b>azimuth of −60 degrees</b> (how far round you stand). The surface is made of <b>900 coloured faces</b>, one for each of the 60 × 60 squares between the 61 × 61 grid points. The axes need labels as always. Everything about the picture (angle, colours, faces) is stored in objects you can read back, which is how this lesson measures a plot instead of only looking at it.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['axes type', 'Axes3D'], ['default elevation / azimuth', '30 / -60 degrees'], ['faces of the surface', '900 = 60 x 60'], ['colours from', 'height (viridis)']], cap: `the picture is an object you can query` } },
    { code: `point = (4.0, 0.0, 1.0)\nfor azim in (-90, -60, 0, 45, 90):\n    ax.view_init(elev=30, azim=azim)\n    fig.canvas.draw()\n    x2, y2, _ = proj3d.proj_transform(*point, ax.get_proj())\n    print(f"azimuth {azim:4d}: the point lands at screen x {x2:6.3f}, y {y2:6.3f}")`, out: `azimuth  -90: the point lands at screen x  0.035, y  0.038\nazimuth  -60: the point lands at screen x  0.031, y  0.030\nazimuth    0: the point lands at screen x  0.000, y  0.021\nazimuth   45: the point lands at screen x -0.026, y  0.026\nazimuth   90: the point lands at screen x -0.035, y  0.038`, say: `<b>The view changes where things land.</b> Take the single point (4, 0, 1) and ask matplotlib where it appears on the <i>screen</i> for five different azimuths. The screen position is <b>x 0.035, y 0.038</b> at −90, x 0.031, y 0.030 at −60, x 0.000, y 0.021 at 0, x −0.026, y 0.026 at 45 and <b>x −0.035, y 0.038</b> at 90. The point has not moved in the data; only the camera has. At azimuth 0 it sits straight above the centre of the screen (x = 0); at −90 and +90 it is at opposite sides. Two points that are far apart in 3D can land on top of each other on screen, and two that are close can look far apart. A fixed 3D picture therefore shows <i>one</i> of many possible flat pictures of your data, and the author chose which.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['azimuth', 'screen x', 'screen y'], rows: [[-90, 0.035, 0.038], [-60, 0.031, 0.03], [0, 0.0, 0.021], [45, -0.026, 0.026], [90, -0.035, 0.038]], cap: `the same point from five viewpoints` } },
    { code: `rng = np.random.default_rng(0)\na = rng.normal([0, 0, 0], 0.6, (60, 3))\nb = rng.normal([2, 2, 0.5], 0.6, (60, 3))\ndef separation(azim, elev=20):\n    ax.view_init(elev=elev, azim=azim)\n    fig.canvas.draw()\n    M = ax.get_proj()\n    pa = np.column_stack(proj3d.proj_transform(a[:, 0], a[:, 1], a[:, 2], M)[:2])\n    pb = np.column_stack(proj3d.proj_transform(b[:, 0], b[:, 1], b[:, 2], M)[:2])\n    return float(np.linalg.norm(pa.mean(0) - pb.mean(0)) / np.sqrt((pa.var(0).sum() + pb.var(0).sum()) / 2))\n\nfor azim in (-90, -60, -30, 0, 30, 60, 90):\n    print(f"azimuth {azim:4d}: the two clusters are {separation(azim):.2f} spreads apart on the screen")`, out: `azimuth  -90: the two clusters are 1.10 spreads apart on the screen\nazimuth  -60: the two clusters are 1.21 spreads apart on the screen\nazimuth  -30: the two clusters are 1.12 spreads apart on the screen\nazimuth    0: the two clusters are 0.77 spreads apart on the screen\nazimuth   30: the two clusters are 0.27 spreads apart on the screen\nazimuth   60: the two clusters are 0.39 spreads apart on the screen\nazimuth   90: the two clusters are 0.87 spreads apart on the screen`, say: `<b>Which angle shows the structure?</b> Two clusters of 60 points each in 3D, centred at (0, 0, 0) and (2, 2, 0.5). Project all the points to the screen at several azimuths and measure how far apart the clusters look, in units of how spread out they are: <b>1.10</b> at −90, <b>1.21 at −60 (the best)</b>, 1.12 at −30, 0.77 at 0, 0.27 at 30 (the <b>worst: the clusters nearly overlap</b>), 0.39 at 60 and 0.87 at 90. The same data look like two clear groups from one side and like a single blob from another. If you want to <b>claim</b> that two groups exist, a rotating view or several fixed views (or a 2D plot of the two best directions) is more honest than one chosen angle. Interactive 3D (drag to rotate) exists exactly for this reason; a printed 3D figure cannot offer it.`, scene: { type: 'series', x: ['-90', '-60', '-30', '0', '30', '60', '90'], lines: [{ name: 'how far apart the clusters look (in spreads)', y: [1.1, 1.21, 1.12, 0.77, 0.27, 0.39, 0.87] }], title: 'separation of the two clusters against the viewing azimuth' } },
    { code: `X2, Y2 = np.meshgrid(np.linspace(-4, 4, 81), np.linspace(-4, 4, 81))\nridge = 3 * np.exp(-(X2 / 0.4) ** 2)\npeak = 2 * np.exp(-((X2 - 2) / 0.4) ** 2 - (Y2 / 0.4) ** 2)\nZ2 = ridge + peak\nfront = Z2.max(axis=1)                      # what you see looking along the x direction: the highest point in each row\nside = Z2.max(axis=0)                       # looking along the y direction: the highest point in each column\ncol = int(np.argmin(np.abs(X2[0] - 2)))\nprint("front view: tallest thing in the row through the peak:", round(float(front[40]), 2), "| the peak (height 2) is hidden behind the ridge:", bool(front.max() <= ridge.max() + 1e-9))\nprint("side view: height seen at x = 2:", round(float(side[col]), 2))`, out: `front view: tallest thing in the row through the peak: 3.0 | the peak (height 2) is hidden behind the ridge: True\nside view: height seen at x = 2: 2.0`, say: `<b>Things hide behind other things.</b> A tall ridge (height 3) runs along x = 0 and a narrow peak (height 2) stands at x = 2, behind it. Looking along the x direction, what you see in each row is the <b>highest</b> point along your line of sight: <b>3.0</b>, the ridge; the peak of height 2 is <b>hidden behind the ridge</b> (the check prints <code>True</code>). Seen from the <b>side</b>, along the y direction, the peak stands clear: its height at x = 2 is <b>2.0</b>. This is <i>occlusion</i>, the biggest practical weakness of 3D charts: features in front hide features behind, and a viewer cannot tell what is missing. The same happens with 3D bar charts, where a short bar vanishes behind a tall one. If exact values or small features matter, prefer a heat map or small multiples (the next lessons).`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['view', 'what you can see'], rows: [['front: along the x direction', 'only the ridge (3.0); the peak of 2.0 is hidden'], ['side: along the y direction', 'the peak stands out at height 2.0']], hlRows: [0], cap: `a 3D picture hides what is behind` } },
    { code: `buf = io.BytesIO()\nfig.savefig(buf, format="png", dpi=80)\ndata = buf.getvalue()\nimg = plt.imread(io.BytesIO(data))\nprint(data[:4], len(data) > 5000, img.shape)\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `b'\\x89PNG' True (320, 400, 4)`, say: `<b>Save it.</b> <code>fig.savefig(buf, format="png", dpi=80)</code> writes the picture as PNG bytes (here into memory). The first four bytes are <b>b'\\x89PNG'</b>, the signature of a PNG file; the data is more than 5,000 bytes; and reading it back with <code>plt.imread</code> gives an array of shape <b>(320, 400, 4)</b>: 320 pixels high, 400 wide (a 5 × 4 inch figure at 80 dots per inch) and 4 channels (red, green, blue, opacity). A saved picture is just a grid of numbers, like any other. Choose the resolution (<code>dpi</code>) for the destination: 80 to 100 for the screen, 200 to 300 for print. And remember to close figures (<code>plt.close(fig)</code>) in loops, or memory fills with plots nobody is looking at.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['file signature (first 4 bytes)', 'PNG'], ['size of the data', 'more than 5,000 bytes'], ['array read back: height, width, channels', '320 x 400 x 4'], ['from', '5 x 4 inches at 80 dpi']], hlRows: [2], cap: `a picture is a grid of numbers` } },
  ],
  quiz: { q: `Why can a single 3D picture mislead?`, opts: [`3D charts are always wrong`, `It shows the data from only one viewing angle; features can overlap on screen or hide behind others, and the author chose the angle`, `The colours are random`, `Matplotlib rounds the data`], a: 1, why: `A 3D chart is one flat projection of the data. Different angles show different structure, and occlusion hides features, so claims should not rest on a single viewpoint.` },
  remember: [`A surface = two coordinate grids from meshgrid and a height grid of the same shape.`, `plot_surface draws it; elevation and azimuth set the view (default 30 and -60 degrees).`, `The same point lands in different screen positions as the view turns; the best angle for a claim is not the only one.`, `Occlusion hides what is behind: prefer heat maps or small multiples when small features or exact values matter.`, `savefig writes a PNG (a grid of numbers); choose dpi for the destination and close figures in loops.`],
  lab: { href: '#/notebook', label: 'Try it in the Notebook' },
});

PIC.lesson({
  id: 'mpl-story', mod: 'matplotlib', les: [4], group: 'Data science and AI', title: 'Seaborn and telling a story', icon: '🎨',
  setup: `import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use("Agg")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nfrom matplotlib.cbook import boxplot_stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nsessions = pd.DataFrame({"plan": np.repeat(["free", "pro", "team"], 200),\n                         "minutes": np.concatenate([rng.gamma(2.0, 4.0, 200), rng.gamma(3.0, 6.0, 200), rng.gamma(4.0, 7.0, 200)]).round(1)})\nsessions["tickets"] = (sessions.minutes * 0.05 + rng.normal(0, 1, 600)).round(2)`,
  sub: 'Use seaborn for statistical charts and check what it draws against NumPy, then turn a chart into an argument: a title that states the finding, one highlighted line, labelled axes, and colours people with colour blindness can tell apart. Runs on your own computer.',
  story: `<b>Seaborn</b> sits on top of matplotlib and draws common statistical charts (histograms, box plots, fitted lines, heat maps) from a table in one line. It is easy to trust a chart you did not construct; so here every chart is checked against numbers computed another way. The second half is about <b>storytelling</b>: a chart that answers a question and says so, with the reader's eye guided to the point, and with colours that survive colour blindness (about 1 man in 12). The data are simulated session lengths for three plans.`,
  steps: [
    { code: `edges = np.linspace(0, 60, 13)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nsns.histplot(data=sessions[sessions.plan == "pro"], x="minutes", bins=edges, ax=ax)\nheights = np.array([p.get_height() for p in ax.patches])\ncounts, _ = np.histogram(sessions[sessions.plan == "pro"].minutes, bins=edges)\nprint(heights.astype(int).tolist())\nprint(np.array_equal(heights, counts), "- the bars are exactly np.histogram counts")\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `[12, 34, 49, 43, 24, 16, 13, 4, 3, 0, 2, 0]\nTrue - the bars are exactly np.histogram counts`, say: `<b>A histogram is counts in bins.</b> <code>sns.histplot</code> draws the minutes per session of the "pro" plan in 12 bins of 5 minutes. The heights of the bars are <b>12, 34, 49, 43, 24, 16, 13, 4, 3, 0, 2, 0</b> sessions, and they are <i>exactly</i> what <code>np.histogram</code> gives for the same bins (<code>True</code>). So a seaborn bar is no mystery: it is a count. The shape is typical of times and amounts: a hump near 10 to 15 minutes and a long tail to the right. The choice of <b>bin width</b> matters: too few bins hide the shape, too many make noise. Draw it a few ways before believing one picture, and state the bin width in the caption.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['minutes per session (bins of 5)', 'sessions'], rows: [['0 to 5', 12], ['5 to 10', 34], ['10 to 15', 49], ['15 to 20', 43], ['20 to 25', 24], ['25 to 30', 16], ['30 to 35 and beyond', '13, 4, 3, 0, 2, 0']], hlRows: [2], cap: `a bar is a count, the same as np.histogram` } },
    { code: `fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nsns.boxplot(data=sessions, x="plan", y="minutes", ax=ax)\nprint(len(ax.patches), "boxes drawn")\nfor plan, g in sessions.groupby("plan"):\n    s = boxplot_stats(g.minutes.values)[0]\n    print(f"{plan:5s} median {s['med']:5.1f} = np.median {np.median(g.minutes):5.1f} | box {s['q1']:5.1f} to {s['q3']:5.1f} | {len(s['fliers'])} points drawn as outliers")\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `3 boxes drawn\nfree  median   6.6 = np.median   6.6 | box   3.8 to  10.4 | 6 points drawn as outliers\npro   median  15.6 = np.median  15.6 | box  10.2 to  21.8 | 5 points drawn as outliers\nteam  median  25.1 = np.median  25.1 | box  18.6 to  35.0 | 6 points drawn as outliers`, say: `<b>A box plot is five numbers.</b> For each plan the box runs from the first to the third quartile with a line at the median, whiskers reach to the furthest ordinary points, and dots mark outliers. The numbers: <b>free</b> median 6.6, box 3.8 to 10.4, 6 outliers; <b>pro</b> median 15.6, box 10.2 to 21.8, 5 outliers; <b>team</b> median 25.1, box 18.6 to 35.0, 6 outliers. The medians equal <code>np.median</code> exactly (matplotlib's own statistics routine gives the same). Read it as a comparison of <i>typical</i> values and of <i>spread</i>: team sessions are longer and more variable. The dots are not errors: with skewed data (long right tails) a few "outliers" are normal; do not delete them without a reason.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['plan', 'median', 'box (middle half)', 'points drawn as outliers'], rows: [['free', 6.6, '3.8 to 10.4', 6], ['pro', 15.6, '10.2 to 21.8', 5], ['team', 25.1, '18.6 to 35.0', 6]], cap: `median and spread in one picture` } },
    { code: `fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nsns.regplot(data=sessions, x="minutes", y="tickets", scatter_kws={"s": 5}, ax=ax)\nline = ax.lines[0].get_xydata()\nslope_plot = (line[-1, 1] - line[0, 1]) / (line[-1, 0] - line[0, 0])\nslope_np = np.polyfit(sessions.minutes, sessions.tickets, 1)[0]\nprint(round(float(slope_plot), 4), round(float(slope_np), 4), np.isclose(slope_plot, slope_np))\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `0.0431 0.0431 True`, say: `<b>A fitted line is a regression.</b> <code>sns.regplot</code> draws the points of minutes against support tickets and a straight line through them, with a band for the uncertainty of the line. The slope of the drawn line is <b>0.0431</b> tickets per minute, identical to the slope from <code>np.polyfit</code> (<b>0.0431</b>, and the check prints <code>True</code>): the line is the ordinary least-squares fit you computed by hand in the statistics lessons. About one extra ticket for every 23 minutes of session. Remember that a line through a cloud shows an association, not a cause, and that the band shows the uncertainty of the <i>line</i>, not the spread of the individual points.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'slope (tickets per minute)'], rows: [['read off the line drawn by seaborn', 0.0431], ['np.polyfit', 0.0431], ['the same?', 'True']], hlRows: [2], cap: `seaborn draws a regression you can reproduce` } },
    { code: `wide = sessions.assign(week=np.tile(np.arange(200), 3)).pivot(index="week", columns="plan", values="minutes")\ncorr = wide.corr().round(2)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nsns.heatmap(corr, annot=True, ax=ax)\nshown = ax.collections[0].get_array().reshape(3, 3)\nprint(corr.values.tolist())\nprint(np.allclose(shown, corr.values), len(ax.texts), "numbers written in the cells")\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `[[1.0, -0.05, -0.05], [-0.05, 1.0, -0.04], [-0.05, -0.04, 1.0]]\nTrue 9 numbers written in the cells`, say: `<b>A heat map is a table with colour.</b> The correlation between the weekly session minutes of the three plans is a 3 × 3 table: 1.0 on the diagonal, and <b>−0.05, −0.05 and −0.04</b> elsewhere: essentially no relationship between plans from week to week. <code>sns.heatmap(corr, annot=True)</code> draws the table as coloured squares (the array behind the picture equals the table: <code>True</code>) and writes <b>9 numbers</b> into the 9 cells. With so few cells the numbers are enough; the colour starts to earn its keep when there are dozens of cells (the next lesson). Always include the numbers or a colour bar: colour alone cannot be read to two decimals.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'free', 'pro', 'team'], rows: [['free', 1.0, -0.05, -0.05], ['pro', -0.05, 1.0, -0.04], ['team', -0.05, -0.04, 1.0]], cap: `a table, coloured` } },
    { code: `weeks = np.arange(1, 13)\nseries = {name: 40 + rng.normal(0, 2, 12).cumsum() * 0.3 for name in ("free", "team", "trial", "legacy")}\nseries["pro"] = 40 + weeks * 2.6\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nfor name, y in series.items():\n    ax.plot(weeks, y, color="#1f5fbf" if name == "pro" else "#bbbbbb", linewidth=3 if name == "pro" else 1)\ngrowth = series["pro"][-1] / series["pro"][0] - 1\nax.set_title(f"Pro sessions grew {growth:.0%} in 12 weeks while the other plans stayed flat")\nax.set_xlabel("week"); ax.set_ylabel("average session minutes")\ncolours = {l.get_color() for l in ax.lines}\nchecks = {"the title states a finding with a number": any(ch.isdigit() for ch in ax.get_title()), "both axes are labelled": bool(ax.get_xlabel() and ax.get_ylabel()),\n          "one highlight colour against grey": len(colours) == 2, "no more than five lines": len(ax.lines) <= 5}\nfor k, v in checks.items():\n    print("PASS" if v else "FAIL", k)\nprint(ax.get_title())\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `PASS the title states a finding with a number\nPASS both axes are labelled\nPASS one highlight colour against grey\nPASS no more than five lines\nPro sessions grew 67% in 12 weeks while the other plans stayed flat`, say: `<b>Turn the chart into an argument.</b> Six lines would be a tangle; here five are grey and one is blue and thick: the eye goes straight to the one that matters. The <b>title states the finding</b>, with its number: "Pro sessions grew 67% in 12 weeks while the other plans stayed flat" (the 67% is computed from the data, so it cannot drift from the chart). The code then checks the chart like a test: <b>PASS</b> the title states a finding with a number; <b>PASS</b> both axes are labelled; <b>PASS</b> one highlight colour against grey; <b>PASS</b> no more than five lines (the checks look at the figure objects). A chart title of "Sessions by plan" makes the reader do the work; a title that says what to notice and a chart that shows it is the difference between a picture and a message.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['check on the figure', 'result'], rows: [['the title states a finding with a number', 'PASS'], ['both axes are labelled', 'PASS'], ['one highlight colour against grey', 'PASS'], ['no more than five lines', 'PASS']], cap: `a chart you can test` } },
    { code: `def to_linear(c):\n    c = np.array(c, float)\n    return np.where(c <= 0.04045, c / 12.92, ((c + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4)\nDEUTAN = np.array([[0.367322, 0.860646, -0.227968], [0.280085, 0.672501, 0.047413], [-0.011820, 0.042940, 0.968881]])\ndef as_seen_by_deuteranope(rgb):\n    return np.clip(DEUTAN @ to_linear(rgb), 0, 1)\ndef closest_pair(palette):\n    seen = [as_seen_by_deuteranope(c) for c in palette]\n    return min(float(np.linalg.norm(seen[i] - seen[j])) for i in range(len(seen)) for j in range(i + 1, len(seen)))\n\ndefault = sns.color_palette("tab10")[:5]\nokabe_ito = [(0, 0.447, 0.698), (0.902, 0.624, 0), (0.337, 0.706, 0.914), (0.835, 0.369, 0), (0.941, 0.894, 0.259)]\nred_green = [(0.84, 0.15, 0.16), (0.17, 0.63, 0.17)]\nprint("closest two colours, as a person with deuteranopia sees them (bigger is easier to tell apart):")\nprint(" tab10 (first five)   ", round(closest_pair(default), 3))\nprint(" Okabe-Ito (five)     ", round(closest_pair(okabe_ito), 3))\nprint(" red and green pair   ", round(closest_pair(red_green), 3))`, out: `closest two colours, as a person with deuteranopia sees them (bigger is easier to tell apart):\n tab10 (first five)    0.067\n Okabe-Ito (five)      0.315\n red and green pair    0.07`, say: `<b>Colours for everyone.</b> About 1 man in 12 has some red-green colour blindness; deuteranopia (no working green cones) is the most common kind. The check converts colours to what such a viewer sees and measures the distance between the <i>closest two</i> colours of a palette (bigger means easier to tell apart): the default <code>tab10</code> palette (first five colours) scores <b>0.067</b>; the <b>Okabe-Ito</b> palette, designed for colour-blind readers, scores <b>0.315</b>, almost five times better. A classic red-and-green pair scores <b>0.07</b>: nearly identical to the viewer. So: avoid red against green as the only distinction; use a colour-blind-safe palette, vary line style or marker as well as colour, label lines directly instead of relying on a legend, and make sure the chart still works in greyscale.`, scene: { type: 'series', x: ['tab10 (5)', 'Okabe-Ito (5)', 'red and green'], lines: [{ name: 'distance between the closest two colours, as a deuteranope sees them', y: [0.067, 0.315, 0.07] }], title: 'the closest pair of colours under deuteranopia (bigger is better)' } },
  ],
  quiz: { q: `A chart is titled "Sessions by plan" and uses red and green lines. What two improvements would make it clearer to everyone?`, opts: [`A bigger font and a border`, `A title that states the finding with a number, and colours that stay distinguishable for colour-blind viewers (or another cue such as line style)`, `More lines`, `A 3D effect`], a: 1, why: `A finding-style title tells the reader what to notice, and colour-blind-safe colours (plus a second cue) keep the message readable for about 8% of men.` },
  remember: [`Seaborn bars, boxes and lines are ordinary statistics: histogram bars equal np.histogram counts, box lines are quartiles, a regplot line is the least-squares fit.`, `Check a chart you did not build against a number you computed another way.`, `State the finding in the title; highlight one thing and grey out the rest; label the axes.`, `Test charts like code: look at the figure objects (title, labels, number of colours).`, `Use colour-blind-safe palettes (Okabe-Ito) and a second cue; avoid red against green.`],
  lab: { href: '#/notebook', label: 'Try it in the Notebook' },
});

PIC.lesson({
  id: 'mpl-heat', mod: 'matplotlib', les: [5], group: 'Data science and AI', title: 'Heatmaps and animated curves', icon: '🔥',
  setup: `import os\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use("Agg")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom matplotlib.colors import Normalize, TwoSlopeNorm\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nblocks = np.repeat([0, 1, 2], 4)\nlatent = rng.normal(size=(300, 3))\ndata = latent[:, blocks] * 0.9 + rng.normal(0, 0.7, (300, 12))\norder0 = rng.permutation(12)\ndata = data[:, order0]                                # the 12 variables, shuffled\nblocks = blocks[order0]\ncorr = np.corrcoef(data.T)`,
  sub: 'Show a correlation matrix as a heat map and check what was drawn, choose a colour map that tells the truth, reorder the rows so the structure appears, centre a diverging scale at zero, build an animated GIF, and know when a few still frames are better. Runs on your own computer.',
  story: `A <b>heat map</b> turns a table of numbers into colour, and is the right tool when a table is too big to read: here a 12 × 12 correlation matrix. Two choices decide whether it tells the truth: the <b>colour map</b> (does brighter really mean bigger?) and the <b>order</b> of the rows (random order hides the pattern). Then the lesson animates a curve, builds a GIF and looks honestly at what an animation costs. Twelve simulated variables hide three groups of four related ones, shuffled so that you cannot see them.`,
  steps: [
    { code: `fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nim = ax.imshow(corr)\nfig.colorbar(im)\nprint(im.get_array().shape, np.allclose(im.get_array(), corr), [round(float(v), 2) for v in im.get_clim()])\nprint(round(float(corr.min()), 2), round(float(np.sort(corr.ravel())[-13]), 2), "- weakest and strongest correlation between two different variables")\nplt.close(fig)`, out: `(12, 12) True [-0.1, 1.0]\n-0.1 0.68 - weakest and strongest correlation between two different variables`, say: `<b>Draw the matrix.</b> <code>ax.imshow(corr)</code> paints each of the 144 cells of the 12 × 12 correlation matrix with a colour. The array behind the image is the matrix itself (shape (12, 12), <code>np.allclose</code> says <b>True</b>), and the colour scale runs from the lowest value, <b>−0.1</b>, to the highest, <b>1.0</b>. Between two <i>different</i> variables the weakest correlation is −0.1 and the strongest <b>0.68</b>. The diagonal is always 1 (a variable with itself). But the picture is unhelpful: with the variables in a random order the squares look like noise and you cannot see that there is structure. The data hold three groups of four related variables; the next steps make them visible.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['cells', '12 x 12 = 144'], ['colour scale from', -0.1], ['colour scale to', 1.0], ['strongest correlation between two different variables', 0.68], ['array behind the picture equals the matrix?', 'True']], cap: `the picture is the matrix, painted` } },
    { code: `def luminance(cmap_name):\n    rgb = plt.get_cmap(cmap_name)(np.linspace(0, 1, 256))[:, :3]\n    return 0.2126 * rgb[:, 0] + 0.7152 * rgb[:, 1] + 0.0722 * rgb[:, 2]\n\nfor name in ("viridis", "gray", "coolwarm", "jet"):\n    lum = luminance(name)\n    turns = int((np.diff(np.sign(np.diff(lum))) != 0).sum())\n    print(f"{name:9s} brightness goes from {lum[0]:.2f} to {lum[-1]:.2f} and changes direction {turns} times")`, out: `viridis   brightness goes from 0.08 to 0.87 and changes direction 0 times\ngray      brightness goes from 0.00 to 1.00 and changes direction 0 times\ncoolwarm  brightness goes from 0.32 to 0.17 and changes direction 1 times\njet       brightness goes from 0.04 to 0.11 and changes direction 3 times`, say: `<b>Choose a colour map that tells the truth.</b> A good <i>sequential</i> map gets steadily brighter as the number rises, so that brightness means "bigger" even in greyscale. The check measures brightness along each map and counts how often it changes direction: <b>viridis</b> goes from 0.08 to 0.87 and never reverses (<b>0</b> turns), <b>gray</b> 0.00 to 1.00 (0 turns), <b>coolwarm</b> (a diverging map: dark at both ends, light in the middle) reverses once, and the rainbow <b>jet</b> reverses <b>3 times</b>: its brightness rises, falls, rises and falls again, so the eye sees bands and edges that are <i>not in the data</i> and cannot tell high from low in a greyscale print. Use viridis-type maps for ordered values, a diverging map only when there is a meaningful centre (zero, an average), and avoid jet.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['colour map', 'brightness from to', 'direction changes', 'use for'], rows: [['viridis', '0.08 to 0.87', 0, 'ordered values'], ['gray', '0.00 to 1.00', 0, 'ordered values, print'], ['coolwarm', '0.32 to 0.17', 1, 'values around a centre'], ['jet (rainbow)', '0.04 to 0.11', 3, 'avoid: invents bands']], hlRows: [3], cap: `brightness should follow the numbers` } },
    { code: `from scipy.cluster.hierarchy import linkage, leaves_list\ndef neighbour_corr(c):\n    return float(np.mean([c[i, i + 1] for i in range(len(c) - 1)]))\n\norder = leaves_list(linkage(1 - corr, method="average"))\nprint("average correlation of neighbouring variables:", round(neighbour_corr(corr), 2), "-> after reordering:", round(neighbour_corr(corr[np.ix_(order, order)]), 2))\nprint("hidden group of each variable, in the new order:", blocks[order].tolist())\nprint("changes of group along the new order:", int((np.diff(blocks[order]) != 0).sum()), "(2 is the minimum for three groups)")`, out: `average correlation of neighbouring variables: 0.13 -> after reordering: 0.5\nhidden group of each variable, in the new order: [2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0]\nchanges of group along the new order: 2 (2 is the minimum for three groups)`, say: `<b>Reorder so the structure appears.</b> The 12 variables came in a random order, and the average correlation between <i>neighbouring</i> variables is only <b>0.13</b>. Hierarchical clustering (<code>linkage</code> and <code>leaves_list</code> from SciPy) finds an order that puts similar variables next to each other. After reordering, neighbours correlate at <b>0.50</b>, and the hidden groups line up as <b>[2, 2, 2, 2, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0]</b>: along the new order the group changes only <b>twice</b>, the minimum for three groups. Drawn with imshow, the reordered matrix shows three bright blocks along the diagonal. The structure was there all along; the order hid it. For any heat map of a similarity matrix (correlations, distances) ordering the rows by clustering is the first thing to do.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'average correlation of neighbouring variables'], rows: [['random order', 0.13], ['after clustering the rows and columns', 0.5]], hlRows: [1], cap: `the order reveals the three groups` } },
    { code: `lo, hi = float(corr.min()), 1.0\nplain = Normalize(lo, hi)\ncentred = TwoSlopeNorm(vcenter=0, vmin=lo, vmax=hi)\nprint("plain scale: zero sits at", round(float(plain(0)), 2), "of the colour range | centred scale: zero sits at", round(float(centred(0)), 2))\ncm = plt.get_cmap("coolwarm")\nprint("colour of zero:", [round(float(v), 2) for v in cm(centred(0))[:3]], "(grey-white = the neutral middle of coolwarm)")\nprint("colour of the lowest value:", [round(float(v), 2) for v in cm(centred(lo))[:3]], "| the highest:", [round(float(v), 2) for v in cm(centred(hi))[:3]])`, out: `plain scale: zero sits at 0.09 of the colour range | centred scale: zero sits at 0.5\ncolour of zero: [0.87, 0.86, 0.86] (grey-white = the neutral middle of coolwarm)\ncolour of the lowest value: [0.23, 0.3, 0.75] | the highest: [0.71, 0.02, 0.15]`, say: `<b>Centre a diverging scale at zero.</b> Correlations range from −0.1 to 1.0. With a plain linear scale, zero sits at <b>0.09</b> of the colour range, almost at the bottom, so "no correlation" shows as a strongly coloured cell. With <code>TwoSlopeNorm(vcenter=0)</code> zero is placed at <b>0.5</b>, the middle of the map, which for <code>coolwarm</code> is the neutral near-white (<b>0.87, 0.86, 0.86</b>); the lowest value is blue (0.23, 0.30, 0.75) and the highest red (0.71, 0.02, 0.15). Now colour carries a second message: red means a positive correlation, blue a negative one, white none. A diverging map without a centred scale <b>lies about where "nothing" is</b>: make the neutral colour mean a number that is actually neutral.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['scale', 'where zero sits (0 to 1)', 'colour of zero'], rows: [['plain linear', 0.09, 'a strong colour: misleading'], ['centred at zero (TwoSlopeNorm)', 0.5, 'near-white: neutral']], hlRows: [1], cap: `the middle colour must mean the middle number` } },
    { code: `from matplotlib.animation import FuncAnimation, PillowWriter\nfrom PIL import Image\nt = np.linspace(0, 10, 200)\ny = np.exp(-0.3 * t) * np.cos(3 * t)\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(4, 2.5))\n(line,) = ax.plot([], [])\nax.set_xlim(0, 10); ax.set_ylim(-1.1, 1.1)\ndef update(i):\n    line.set_data(t[:i * 5 + 1], y[:i * 5 + 1])\n    return (line,)\n\nanim = FuncAnimation(fig, update, frames=40, interval=50, blit=True)\nanim.save("damped.gif", writer=PillowWriter(fps=20))\ngif = Image.open("damped.gif")\nprint(gif.n_frames, "frames,", gif.info.get("duration"), "ms each, size", gif.size)\nprint("the last frame draws all", len(t), "points; the first draws", 1)`, out: `40 frames, 50 ms each, size (400, 250)\nthe last frame draws all 200 points; the first draws 1`, say: `<b>An animated curve.</b> A damped oscillation, <code>exp(−0.3 t) × cos(3 t)</code>, drawn a little further in each frame by <code>FuncAnimation</code>, 40 frames, then saved as a GIF with the Pillow writer. Reading the file back: <b>40 frames</b>, <b>50 milliseconds each</b> (20 frames a second), size <b>400 × 250</b> pixels (a 4 × 2.5 inch figure at 100 dpi). The first frame draws 1 point and the last draws all 200. The function <code>update(i)</code> is the whole animation: it receives the frame number and sets what to show; matplotlib calls it 40 times. (Saving a GIF needs Pillow and runs on your own computer, not in the in-browser Python.) Animation is natural for something that <i>unfolds</i> in time, like this curve being traced.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'value'], rows: [['frames in the GIF', 40], ['time per frame', '50 ms (20 per second)'], ['picture size', '400 x 250'], ['points drawn in the first and last frame', '1 and 200']], cap: `update(i) is the whole animation` } },
    { code: `fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 4, figsize=(8, 2))\nfor ax, frame in zip(axes, (0, 13, 26, 39)):\n    ax.plot(t[:frame * 5 + 1], y[:frame * 5 + 1]); ax.set_xlim(0, 10); ax.set_ylim(-1.1, 1.1); ax.set_title(f"step {frame}", fontsize=8)\nfig.savefig("static.png", dpi=80)\nplt.close(fig)\nsizes = {"static 4-panel PNG": os.path.getsize("static.png"), "40-frame GIF": os.path.getsize("damped.gif")}\nprint({k: v > 0 for k, v in sizes.items()}, "| the GIF is larger than the PNG:", sizes["40-frame GIF"] > sizes["static 4-panel PNG"])\nprint("a GIF cannot be paused, searched, printed or read by a screen reader; four frames side by side can")`, out: `{'static 4-panel PNG': True, '40-frame GIF': True} | the GIF is larger than the PNG: True\na GIF cannot be paused, searched, printed or read by a screen reader; four frames side by side can`, say: `<b>When not to animate.</b> The four-panel still picture (frames 0, 13, 26 and 39 side by side) and the 40-frame GIF both exist; the GIF is the <b>larger file</b> (the check prints <code>True</code>). More important than size: a GIF cannot be paused at the moment you want, searched, printed in a report, quoted, or read aloud by a screen reader, and the viewer must wait for the part that matters. Four frames side by side (small multiples) let the eye compare any two moments at once and print fine. Animation earns its place when the <b>motion itself</b> is the message (a simulation, a process unfolding) or in a live presentation; for a report, an analysis or anything that must be compared, show still frames or a static chart with the whole curve and mark the interesting points.`, scene: { type: 'table', cols: ['', 'animated GIF', 'four frames side by side'], rows: [['can be paused and compared', 'no', 'yes'], ['prints in a report', 'no', 'yes'], ['readable by a screen reader', 'no', 'yes (with a caption)'], ['file size', 'larger', 'smaller'], ['best for', 'a process that unfolds, live talks', 'analysis and reports']], cap: `choose the form that fits the message` } },
  ],
  quiz: { q: `Why is a rainbow colour map such as jet a poor choice for a heat map of ordered values?`, opts: [`It uses too many colours`, `Its brightness goes up and down, so the eye sees false bands and edges and cannot tell high from low in greyscale`, `It is too dark`, `It is slow to draw`], a: 1, why: `A good sequential map brightens steadily with the value. jet reverses direction several times, which creates features that are not in the data.` },
  remember: [`imshow paints a matrix; check that the array behind the image equals your table.`, `Use viridis-type maps for ordered values; a diverging map only for a meaningful centre; avoid jet.`, `Reorder rows and columns by clustering (linkage, leaves_list) so that blocks appear.`, `Centre a diverging scale with TwoSlopeNorm(vcenter=0) so the neutral colour means zero.`, `FuncAnimation calls update(i) per frame; animate only when the motion is the message, otherwise use small multiples.`],
  lab: { href: '#/notebook', label: 'Try it in the Notebook' },
});
